WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.179
You have seen this. A wet road at sunset, glowing in a long streak of glare.

2
00:00:05.529 --> 00:00:11.310
Or a metal floor in a game that turns grainy and sparkly the moment the camera drops low.

3
00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:16.289
Both are rough, shiny surfaces seen at a grazing angle, almost edge on.

4
00:00:16.639 --> 00:00:23.167
So here is the puzzle. Why does a renderer struggle most exactly there, with the same number of samples?

5
00:00:23.517 --> 00:00:29.469
By the end, the answer will be one simple idea. Only sample what the viewer can actually see.

6
00:00:30.669 --> 00:00:34.360
Here is the far lip of a steel sluice gate, rendered twice.

7
00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:38.401
Same scene, same thirty-two samples per pixel, same seed.

8
00:00:38.751 --> 00:00:42.271
The only difference is how we choose microfacet normals.

9
00:00:42.621 --> 00:00:49.127
On the left we sample the distribution of normals. On the right, the distribution of visible normals.

10
00:00:49.477 --> 00:00:53.168
Let's build up, step by step, why the right side is cleaner.

11
00:00:54.368 --> 00:00:59.573
Zoom far enough into rough metal, and the surface becomes a field of tiny mirrors.

12
00:00:59.923 --> 00:01:04.979
Each mirror has its own normal, which we call h. The big surface normal is n.

13
00:01:05.329 --> 00:01:11.751
G G X describes how those little normals are spread around n, with a density called D of h.

14
00:01:12.101 --> 00:01:18.458
Here is D of h written out. Alpha is the roughness. For this gate, alpha is zero point two eight.

15
00:01:18.808 --> 00:01:23.779
Larger alpha spreads the facets wider. Smaller alpha keeps them close to n.

16
00:01:24.979 --> 00:01:29.203
The textbook sampler is simple. Draw a facet normal h from D.

17
00:01:29.553 --> 00:01:34.097
Then reflect the view direction v about h, to get the light direction ell.

18
00:01:34.447 --> 00:01:38.521
Repeat this many times, and we get a fan of reflected directions.

19
00:01:38.871 --> 00:01:41.645
But D knows nothing about where the camera is.

20
00:01:41.995 --> 00:01:48.971
Tilt the view, and some of these reflected directions fall below the horizon. Those samples are simply wasted.

21
00:01:50.171 --> 00:01:55.269
There is one more subtlety. We draw half vectors, but we integrate over light directions.

22
00:01:55.619 --> 00:02:01.827
Take a small patch of half vector directions. Reflecting through it gives a patch of light directions.

23
00:02:02.177 --> 00:02:08.983
That patch is bigger. Its area grows by a factor of four times the absolute value of v dot h.

24
00:02:09.333 --> 00:02:12.149
So the density of ell is the density of h.

25
00:02:12.499 --> 00:02:16.659
Divided by four times the absolute value of v dot h.

26
00:02:17.009 --> 00:02:24.966
We checked this numerically. Drop the factor, or use n dot h instead, and the histogram no longer matches the target.

27
00:02:26.166 --> 00:02:31.371
Now let's tilt the viewer toward grazing, and count facets that face away from v.

28
00:02:31.721 --> 00:02:34.985
Those facets cost a sample and contribute nothing.

29
00:02:35.335 --> 00:02:42.866
At eighty-two degrees, with alpha zero point two eight, about twenty-eight percent of naive draws are back facing.

30
00:02:43.216 --> 00:02:46.075
Sampling visible normals brings that to zero.

31
00:02:47.275 --> 00:02:51.307
The fix is to sample only the facets the viewer can actually see.

32
00:02:51.657 --> 00:02:59.763
Each facet counts in proportion to its area as seen from v, which is v dot h, and back facing facets count zero.

33
00:03:00.113 --> 00:03:08.988
Written out, D sub v of h given v equals G one of v, times the max of zero and v dot h, times D of h.

34
00:03:09.338 --> 00:03:16.805
All divided by n dot v. G one is Smith's masking term, and it makes the whole thing integrate to one.

35
00:03:17.155 --> 00:03:23.277
At normal incidence this is the same as before. At grazing, the lobe tilts toward the viewer.

36
00:03:24.477 --> 00:03:32.221
How do we draw from it? First, stretch the scene so the rough surface becomes a perfect hemisphere, with alpha equal to one.

37
00:03:32.571 --> 00:03:38.843
A hemispherical mirror lit by a parallel beam reflects it uniformly into a spherical cap.

38
00:03:39.193 --> 00:03:42.287
So we sample a point on that cap, which is easy.

39
00:03:42.637 --> 00:03:48.013
Then unstretch, and the half vector lands exactly on the visible normal distribution.

40
00:03:48.363 --> 00:03:54.827
This is the Dupuy and Benyoub construction, and it matches Heitz's original to within sampling noise.

41
00:03:56.027 --> 00:04:05.307
Now look at the sample weight. For naive sampling it is F times G two, times v dot h, over n dot v times n dot h.

42
00:04:05.657 --> 00:04:17.177
That ratio has no upper bound. At eighty-two degrees, about eighteen percent of naive weights exceed one, and the ninety-ninth percentile is about three point seven five.

43
00:04:17.527 --> 00:04:24.268
For visible normals, almost everything cancels. The weight is just F times G two over G one.

44
00:04:24.618 --> 00:04:34.730
And G two over G one never exceeds one. So the weight never exceeds F. Across the whole sweep, the largest weight was about zero point seven.

45
00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:48.904
And that is the aha. The grain on a low angle metal floor comes from wasted samples, plus rare samples with huge weights. Sampling only the visible normals removes the wasted draws and caps the weights.

46
00:04:50.104 --> 00:04:52.323
Here is what that buys us, measured.

47
00:04:52.673 --> 00:04:58.091
At normal incidence, the two samplers are identical. The ratio is exactly one.

48
00:04:58.441 --> 00:05:08.852
At seventy-five degrees, visible normals cut the standard deviation by three point five times. At eighty-two degrees, by five point seven times.

49
00:05:09.202 --> 00:05:12.871
In variance, that is roughly twelve times, and thirty-two times.

50
00:05:14.071 --> 00:05:15.629
Now the honest twist.

51
00:05:15.979 --> 00:05:23.403
In the ray-traced scene, the grazing band is only one point six times cleaner, short of the two times we asked for.

52
00:05:23.753 --> 00:05:29.491
Real reflections bring scene variance that a better B R D F sampler cannot remove.

53
00:05:29.841 --> 00:05:38.076
And with an eight times brighter sun right on the mirror direction, visible normals come out slightly worse, at zero point nine three.

54
00:05:38.426 --> 00:05:44.805
When one bright light dominates, the fix is light sampling, combined with multiple importance sampling.

55
00:05:46.005 --> 00:05:47.349
So, the takeaway.

56
00:05:47.699 --> 00:05:59.261
Sample visible normals, apply the half vector Jacobian correctly, and the weight stays bounded. Today this is the standard way modern path tracers sample rough reflections.

57
00:05:59.611 --> 00:06:04.006
At grazing angles, that turns wasted draws into clean pixels.

58
00:06:04.356 --> 00:06:11.097
For the details, see Heitz, twenty eighteen, and Dupuy and Benyoub, twenty twenty-three. Thanks for watching.
